{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a sustained rally, the model level is systematically too low across the whole horizon (often by ~+2 to +10 EUR, growing with lead time). Apply an additive uplift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Forecast path is too flat/mean-reverting: the model tends to pull prices back toward ~70–73 even when the realized path keeps trending to ~80–83. Reduce mean-reversion strength; keep slope aligned with recent momentum.",
    "Lag in regime shifts: after a breakout (e.g., moving from high-60s/low-70s into mid/high-70s), the model follows with delay and never fully catches up. Use a breakout detector (e.g., close > prior 20d high or > MA20 by threshold) to shift the baseline up immediately.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: errors widen materially after ~10 trading days; week-3/4 forecasts are the most biased low. Use a lead-time scaling factor on the correction (monotone increasing with horizon).",
    "Late-horizon reversal risk: in the one case where the true series rolls over at the end (Example 5), the model remains too high at the tail. Add a conditional tail adjustment: if recent momentum weakens (e.g., last 3–5 day returns decelerate or turn negative), taper the uplift beyond ~day 20 to avoid overestimating the final week."
  ]
}
